FORWARD over it, with a control flow dropped in the same leg; (3) behave as **version 2** by
default and differently and correctly as **version 1**, with the difference visible IN THE
PROTOCOL'S OWN PACKETS and not only in a `show`; (4) stop forwarding when the advertisement is
withdrawn, proven on the wire; (5) be removable from the dataplane; (6) survive a reboot.


5. BENCH, AND THIS CASE'S ONE DEVIATION
----------------------------------------
Same shared B/C/D underlay as T43857 (campaign queue, "## Group B/C/D shared L3 underlay and
restore"), verified intact at 21:30-21:32 and again after T43857's teardown at 21:59-22:00.
Group C adds nothing at the bench level.

**DEVIATION, this case only, removed by its own teardown (22:04:35):**
    x960-28YSQ(config)# interface lo
    x960-28YSQ(config-if)# ip address 192.168.70.1/24 secondary
    x960-28YSQ# show ip route
    C       192.168.70.0/24 is directly connected, lo       <- the new prefix
    C       10.70.70.1/32 is directly connected, lo
  (`show ip interface brief` still shows only `lo 10.70.70.1/32` -- secondary addresses are
   invisible there, platforms/x960.md §7.7.  Confirmed here on a second model.)

**WHY a second prefix is needed, and it is the heart of the v1 half of this case.**  RIPv1
carries **no subnet masks**; a receiver applies the classful mask of the address.  swi_b's
existing loopback `10.70.70.1/32` is a host route inside class A `10.0.0.0/8`, so it cannot
survive a v1 advertisement intact -- and `10/8` also contains both transit /30s, so a classful
v1 advertisement of it would collide with the underlay.  `192.168.70.0/24` is class C, whose
**natural mask IS /24**, so it crosses v1 unchanged and the v1 leg measures RIP rather than
measuring a mask collision.  The CONTROL flow is likewise `203.0.113.9`, deliberately outside
`10/8` for the same reason.


