    the `Timeout after 180 seconds` this platform reports.  Confirmed by the DUT's own view
    (22:15:56):
      awplus# show ip protocols rip
        **Default version control: send version 1, receive version 1**
          vlan10 1 1 · vlan41 1 1 · vlan48 1 1 · vlan61 1 1
        Routing Information Sources:
          Gateway       Distance  Last Update  Bad Packets  Bad Routes
          **10.0.61.2**    120    00:00:27         0            0
                       <- 10.0.41.2 has DROPPED OUT of the sources list entirely
        Number of routes (including connected): 6     (was 7)
    A version-1 receiver does not accept version-2 updates, so the v2-only neighbour ages out.
    **Bad Packets and Bad Routes both stayed 0** -- the v2 updates are not counted as errors,
    they are simply not accepted.

  3d. WHAT SURVIVED v1, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN INCONSISTENCY.  `10.70.70.1/32` -- a /32 -- is
    STILL learned from swi_b under v1, and is still being refreshed (`show ip rip database`
    22:15:57: `R 10.70.70.1/32  10.0.61.2  2  10.0.61.2  vlan61  02:32`, timer resetting).
    That is correct RIPv1: swi_b advertises it on vlan61, whose own address 10.0.61.1/30 is in
    the SAME major network 10.0.0.0/8, so the entry is not summarised at a class boundary and a
    RIPv1 receiver reads an address with a non-zero host part as a HOST route.  The DUT could
    only have derived the /32 that way, because v1 carries no mask -- so the /32 in the database
    IS the evidence of the host-route rule.  **The classful collapse seen in 3b happens on
    vlan10, a different major network; the two observations are the same rule applied to two
    interfaces, not a contradiction.**

