    22:21:28          database: metric 16, **01:21**
    22:21:43          database: metric 16, **01:06**
    22:21:58          database: metric 16, **00:51**
    22:22:13          **gone from the database entirely**
    Throughout, from the SAME neighbour on the SAME interface:
      R 10.70.70.1/32  10.0.61.2  **metric 2**  vlan61  02:35-02:53 (timer resetting = refreshed)

    swi_b sent a **triggered update at metric 16 (infinity)**, so the DUT removed the route from
    the FIB within 13 s rather than waiting out the 180 s timeout, and then held it in the
    database for the documented **garbage-collect 120 s** (01:48 down to 00:00, measured) before
    deleting it.  Both timers match `show ip protocols rip`: *"Timeout after 180 seconds,
    garbage collect after 120 seconds"*.  **A tester who only ran `show ip route rip` would see
    the route vanish and never learn that it was poisoned rather than aged** -- the two look
    identical in the route table and take 13 s and 180 s respectively.
    (Contrast step 3c, where swi_a's prefix was NOT poisoned -- nobody withdrew it, the DUT
     simply stopped accepting its v2 updates -- and it took the full 180 s timeout.  The two
     mechanisms were both measured in this one case.)

