  🔴 7a. THE F-3 CONDITION, HIT DELIBERATELY AND MEASURED -- AND IT SHARPENS F-3.
    Immediately after the reboot, read twice (22:29:22 and 22:29:47):
      awplus# show arp
      IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
      awplus#                              <- **COMPLETELY EMPTY**
    So all three RIP routes were in the FIB pointing at next hops the DUT had never resolved.
    This is sharper than T43861's F-3 case: **RIPv2 talks to 224.0.0.9 and needs no ARP at
    all**, so nothing primes the next hop -- whereas in T43857 forming the OSPF adjacency
    resolved both next hops for free.  The textbook F-3 worry applies here in full.

    Rather than priming it first, the leg was run deliberately against the COLD table:
      tool: as step 1, --tag M7-after-reload-coldarp.   cap 49 frame(s)
      10.70.70.1   : 5 sent, **5 echo replies sourced by 10.70.70.1**
      192.168.70.1 : 5 sent, **5 echo replies sourced by 192.168.70.1**
      10.90.90.1   : 5 sent, **5 echo replies sourced by 10.90.90.1**
      203.0.113.9  : 5 sent, 0 replies -- control still dropped after the reboot
      192.168.10.1 : 5 sent, 5 replies -- instrument alive
      awplus# show arp                                           (22:30:28, after the leg)
      192.168.10.5  0011.2233.4401  vlan10  port1.0.10  dynamic
      10.0.61.2     0000.cd42.3029  vlan61  port1.0.16  dynamic
      10.0.41.2     889d.98df.2602  vlan41  port1.0.4   dynamic

    **15 of 15, with not one frame lost, and the ARP table filled by the traffic itself.**  The
    DUT resolved both next hops on demand fast enough that even the FIRST packet of each flow
    got through.
    ⇒ **This narrows F-3 usefully.**  F-3 (platforms/x960.md §7.3) says an ECMP next hop with
    unresolved ARP is silently absent from the dataplane while `show ip route database` still
    marks it `*>`.  Measured here, a **single-path** route with an unresolved next hop is NOT
    affected -- it resolves on demand and forwards everything.  So the F-3 hazard is specific to
    **ECMP group membership**, not to next-hop resolution generally.  A future tester should
    check `show arp` before believing an ECMP route group, but need not do so before a
    single-path leg.  (Recorded for platforms/x960.md as a refinement of §7.3, not a
    contradiction of it -- T43861's measurement stands.)

  🔎 7b. F-5 RESOLVED -- `no service ospf` DOES NOT CLEAR, AND NOW WE KNOW WHY.
    After this second reboot, from a config file that CONTAINS `no service ospf`:
      awplus# show running-config | include service ospf  ->  no service ospf   (still there)
      awplus# show process | include ospf                 ->  **NOTHING -- ospfd is NOT running**
    Compare T43857's teardown, where `no service ospf` appeared WHILE ospfd was still running.
    So the two observations together settle the mechanism: the line is **a plain configuration
    line that `no service ospf` writes into the config**, and once it is in a config file it is
    carried through every subsequent boot of that file.  It is NOT a dynamic "daemon still
    running" indicator -- ospfd is gone and the line remains.  Why it never appeared on swi_b or
    swi_a is then simply that they still hold the DEFAULT (no line at all), and `no service
    ospf` on a device whose running-config was generated from a file containing `service ospf`
    records the negation instead of reverting to the default.
    **Consequence, and it is the actionable half:** a `no service <proto>` line, once written,
    can only be cleared by booting a config that never mentioned the service.  On this bench
    that means the group-C restore cannot remove it by configuration alone.  Declared in
    section 8 and escalated to the Test Engineer at hand-back.

  STEP 7 VERDICT: PASS.  RIP survives a real 61 s reboot: configuration, service gate, version
  control, both neighbours, all three learned routes and the forwarding all return unaided.


