RUN END: 2026-10-15 22:35 NZDT (2026-10-15 09:35 UTC)

VERDICT: PASS -- RIP on the x960-56YSQ is gated behind `service rip`, installs routes LEARNED
FROM THE PROTOCOL (code `R`, AD 120, both neighbours named in `Routing Information Sources`
with 0 bad packets and 0 bad routes) for three prefixes reachable no other way -- no static
route exists at any point -- and FORWARDS over all three 5/5 on the wire while a control flow
to an unrouted prefix is dropped and the instrument control answers in every one of the eight
legs.  **Both versions were proven in the protocol's own packets, not in a `show`:** the
default is RIPv2 to multicast 224.0.0.9 at TTL 1 with an explicit prefix length, a route tag
and a next-hop field on every entry (confirming the guide's "By default, RIP only sends and
receives RIPv2 packets"); `version 1` on the same wire 34 s later is RIPv1 to broadcast
192.168.10.255 at ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff and TTL 64, with no masks, no tags and the four 10/8
entries collapsed into a single classful `10.0.0.0` -- and the host-route rule proven
separately by the /32 that survived v1 on an interface in the same major network.  The version
change had a selective, measurable effect on traffic: swi_a, left at v2, timed out after
exactly the documented 180 s, dropped out of the sources list with 0 bad packets, and went
5/5 -> 0/5 in the dataplane while the two v1 prefixes stayed 5/5 in the same leg; returning to
v2 recovered it in ~6 s.  Withdrawal was proven on the wire with the tightest control the bench
allows -- one prefix withdrawn while the other prefix FROM THE SAME NEIGHBOUR OVER THE SAME
PORT kept forwarding -- and the mechanism was caught in the act: a triggered update at
metric 16 took the route out of the FIB in 13 s, then the documented 120 s garbage-collect
timer ran it out of the database, which `show ip route` alone could never have distinguished
from a 180 s timeout.  `no router rip` returns the dataplane to the step-1 baseline, and the
whole feature survives a real 61 s reboot.  Two findings recorded, neither a failure of RIP:
F-5 is resolved and explained (section 8, step 7b), and T43861's F-3 is usefully NARROWED --
a single-path route with a completely cold ARP table forwarded 15 of 15 and filled its own ARP
cache, so the F-3 hazard belongs to ECMP group membership, not to next-hop resolution.
